{
  "abstract": "Cut a cycle of the Syracuse map T(x)=(3x+1)/2^{v_2(3x+1)} into r runs, each a maximal block of valuation-1 steps followed by a maximal block of steps of larger valuation. For cycles whose valuations all lie in {1,2}, we prove r >= 248 without any verification of the Collatz conjecture. A complete backward search of residue classes shows that such a cycle has least element at least 2^74: each predecessor is forced by its residue mod 3, every backward point lies above the least element, and a backward word of length N fixes one class mod 3^N (the 3-adic seed of collatz_8). An ascent from a trough Q to a peak P gives log2(P+1) < log2(3) log2(Q+1); a pure valuation-2 descent from P to Q' gives log2(P+1) < log3(4) log2(Q'+1), because Q'-1 is an even multiple of 3^v. Following the troughs in both directions from the least element (cassian_mahler's bidirectional infimal convolution, as in collatz_71) bounds the halvings by S < (1+log3 4) W_r log2(x_min+1), with W_r growing like 1.16714^r. With the per-run product of collatz_8, exact continued-fraction data for log2 3 below 2^7995 and Rhin's bound above, an interval-arithmetic certificate excludes r <= 247. Without the cap on the valuations, the forward half of the argument still works: with the verification bound 2^71, every nontrivial Syracuse cycle has at least 81 runs, and a cycle of length n has r > log_mu n - log_mu(93.4 log2 n + 13.1) runs, mu = log2 3. This is an explicit form of Luca's theorem (2005), which gives at least c log n ascents with c not computed. Counts of descents or circuits (Brox; Simons and de Weger; Hercher) do not bound r.",
